diff --git a/docs/next/swift-runtime.md b/docs/next/swift-runtime.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..b23a639
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/next/swift-runtime.md
@@ -0,0 +1,136 @@
+# Swift runtime extension
+
+This custom build is based on Herdr v0.9.5 (`2ecf19a92fc3a7fff75b6ce3655ef04f4625ec01`).
+It keeps the existing private protocol and generation-1 codecs unchanged. It is
+not an upstream Herdr release. Swift manages its installation and updates.
+
+The local JSON API advertises `capabilities.terminal_state_version: 1` in `ping`.
+Authentication and remote transport remain the gateway's responsibility; the
+native API retains its local socket permissions.
+
+## Terminal state
+
+`terminal.state.subscribe` accepts `{ "terminal_id": "..." }` and holds the
+connection open. It observes the live screen, independently of the TUI's scroll
+position, focus and dimensions. It does not grant input or resize authority.
+
+Each NDJSON result has type `terminal_state`, version 1, `terminal_id`, `boot_id`,
+`revision`, `base_revision`, `cols`, `rows`, `total_rows`, `first_row`, `cursor`,
+`alternate_screen`, `mouse_tracking`, `mouse_sgr`, `mouse_pixels`,
+`bracketed_paste`, and `changes`. Cursor is `[column, row, visible]`. Optional
+`cursor_shape` carries DECSCUSR appearance 0..6, read through a narrow Ghostty
+getter without allocating or updating a render viewport. A change is
+`[row_index, row]`; row indices are relative to the live screen. Each row contains
+`cells`, `soft_wrapped`, and `wrap_continuation`.
+
+A null base revision replaces the entire display state and includes every row.
+Otherwise the client must have exactly the specified base revision and boot
+identity. Reconnect starts from a fresh snapshot. Cursor and mode changes may
+arrive with an empty changes array. Revisions are opaque ordered integers, not
+frame counts; clients must not assume they are consecutive.
+
+Each cell is `[text, width, foreground, background, attributes, underline_color,
+hyperlink]`. Width is 1 for narrow, 2 for wide, 0 for a wide tail and 3 for a wrap
+spacer. Colours are -1 for the default, 0..255 for palette entries, and
+`0x1000000 | rgb` for true colour. Attribute bits 0..7 represent bold, italic,
+faint, blink, inverse, invisible, strikethrough, overline; bits 8..10 hold the
+underline style. Hyperlink is a URI or null. This is parsed terminal state, not
+application output bytes. Future incompatible shapes require a new version.
+
+Snapshots share the native content lock and one cached result per terminal
+revision. Serialization and socket writes happen after releasing the content
+lock. Slow sockets do not queue every intermediate frame; the next write is
+computed from the most recent complete terminal state and the last sent state.
+The existing socket write timeout bounds a stalled connection. Closing a
+terminal ends its observations without transferring process ownership.
+
+## History
+
+State frames also carry `history_epoch`, `row_offset`, `history_start` and
+`history`. Logical row positions are physical screen rows plus `row_offset`.
+The initial snapshot includes up to 200 earlier rows; subsequent snapshots carry
+up to 256 newly historical rows. Missing ranges can be fetched separately.
+
+One terminal-owned Ghostty tracked grid reference anchors the live screen. Its
+position follows output and page pruning, so clients retain independent reading
+positions without comparing text. Resize/reflow, screen changes and discarded
+references start a new epoch. An epoch belongs to the stated boot and terminal.
+
+`terminal.history.read` accepts `terminal_id`, `boot_id`, `revision`, `start`,
+`limit`, and optional `history_epoch`. Without an epoch, physical row positions
+and exact revision checks retain the initial API's behavior. With an epoch,
+`start` is a logical row position and only rows before the live screen are read;
+ordinary newer output does not invalidate the read. A changed epoch or a pruned
+start fails instead of returning unrelated rows. The result includes the request
+identity, epoch, start, and styled `content`. Requests are capped at 2,048 rows
+and 262,144 cells. Reading never changes the native viewport.
+
+The Swift gateway negotiates this interface and forwards bounded chunks through
+its existing multiplexed WebSocket. The browser caches each terminal's native
+state and history and writes only changed visible rows. Existing ANSI clients
+remain compatible. Production migration and full platform performance
+acceptance remain separate work before the overall refactor ships.
+
+## Event delivery
+
+The existing events API wakes on EventHub notification, with its previous
+timeout retained for subscriptions that inspect output or scrolling. JSON
+request setup waits for socket readability rather than sleeping a fixed 100 ms
+after finding no bytes. Event formats and existing subscription semantics are
+preserved.
+
+## Native key encoding and control readiness
+
+`ping` also advertises `capabilities.terminal_input_version: 1`. A pending
+protocol-22 terminal connection may send the existing `EndpointControl` message
+with kind `terminal.control.configure` and JSON data `{ "frames": false }`, then
+resize and acquire control through the existing control request. Configuration
+is accepted only before binding. The runtime applies geometry and sends an
+`EndpointControl` response of kind `terminal.control.ready`, containing
+`terminal_id`, `cols` and `rows`. This controller does not become a render target
+and does not require a rendered frame before accepting input.
+
+The existing `ClientShellPaneInput` message also accepts semantic key events
+from a bound direct terminal controller. The requested pane must resolve to its
+bound terminal; pending clients, observers and other terminal targets cannot use
+this path. The runtime's existing Ghostty encoder applies current keyboard modes,
+modifiers, generated text and press/repeat/release semantics. Held releases are
+tracked by terminal identity and synthesized on disconnect. Mouse input retains
+the existing direct terminal mouse path. No protocol-22 enum or frozen endpoint
+codec shape changes. Swift negotiates this capability and keeps the legacy raw
+input path for old runtimes, browser composition and clipboard input.
+
+## Live handoff identity
+
+The handoff runtime record carries an optional `terminal_id`. Custom runtime
+exports always populate it, and restore reuses it instead of allocating a new
+identity. Persisted ordinary session restore retains its existing semantics.
+
+For a source such as v0.9.3 that predates this field, Swift's migration launcher
+supplies `SWIFT_HERDR_HANDOFF_IDENTITIES` only to the import process. The private
+map binds the source version, protocol, server PID and socket directory to a
+complete, unique PTY PID-to-terminal-ID mapping. Import checks it before
+accepting file descriptors or ownership, rejects mismatches, and removes the
+environment variable. It cannot silently replace an identity already present
+in the native manifest. Subsequent custom-to-custom handoffs need no map.
+
+Swift's migration command verifies the candidate against its build receipt and
+records PTY process start times and Pane/terminal identities before and after
+handoff. It sends no terminal input and never repeats a handoff whose response
+may have been lost. The official system binary stays unchanged. Identity checks
+do not claim that upstream handoff exports every transient rendering or history
+state; that continuity is verified separately before production migration.
+
+
+Custom-to-custom handoff now carries complete Ghostty binary snapshots in private
+0700/0600 temporary files, removed after restore acknowledgment or rollback.
+Readers pause before capture; restoring both screens, history, cursor, modes
+and unfinished VT/UTF-8 precedes ownership commit. Decode or capture failure
+aborts the handoff and resumes the original readers. Imported geometry is kept
+until a real controller resizes it. The bounded ANSI field remains solely as
+legacy importer fallback. The snapshot codec is pinned with the vendored
+Ghostty revision and is not a promised stable public interchange format.
+
+Sources predating this extension cannot export full snapshots. Their limited
+history replay and application redraw remain separate migration constraints;
+preserving process identity does not retroactively recover missing old state.
